    "title": "Direct Elections Held in Galmudug and Hirshabelle Across 23 Districts",
    "original_title": "Doorashooyinka Galmudug Iyo Hirshabeelle: Tijaabada Qof Iyo Codka Soomaaliya",
    "slug": "direct-elections-held-in-galmudug-and-hirshabelle-across-23-districts",
    "summary": "Residents in Galmudug and Hirshabelle states voted on Tuesday to directly elect local council and parliamentary representatives in 23 districts, marking a significant step toward direct elections in Somalia.",
    "content": "Voters in Somalia's Galmudug and Hirshabelle regional states went to the polls on Tuesday to directly elect representatives for local councils and regional parliaments. The elections took place across 23 districts: 11 in Galmudug and 12 in Hirshabelle, with Hirshabelle's districts split equally between the Hiiraan and Middle Shabelle regions. This exercise is part of Somalia's broader effort to transition from indirect to direct elections.\n\nThe move follows years of debate over Somalia's electoral model. Since 2012, elections have been conducted through a complex system of clan delegates, which many Somalis criticized as unrepresentative and prone to manipulation. In recent years, the federal government and regional states have committed to holding direct elections, starting at the local level. Galmudug and Hirshabelle are among the first to implement this approach, with support from international partners.\n\nKey figures involved include the leadership of both regional states, who have endorsed the process, and the federal government, which has backed the transition. The elections are seen as a test of Somalia's ability to conduct credible, direct voting amid ongoing security and logistical challenges. The United Nations and other international partners have provided technical and financial assistance.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these elections are particularly significant. Many diaspora members have family ties to these regions and have long advocated for a more inclusive political system that gives citizens a direct say in governance. The shift to direct elections could eventually allow diaspora participation, although current voting is limited to residents. The outcome will be closely watched as a indicator of whether similar exercises can be held elsewhere in Somalia, including in areas with large diaspora populations.\n\nDespite the significance, the elections face hurdles. Al-Shabaab, which opposes the federal government, has threatened to disrupt the process, and logistical challenges remain. However, the peaceful conduct of voting in many areas has been praised as a positive development. The results are expected to be announced in the coming days.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/30/doorashooyinka-galmudug-iyo-hirshabeelle-tijaabada-qof-iyo-codka-soomaaliya/",
