    "summary": "Former South West State President Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed Laftagareen alleges that six missiles struck Baidoa, killing civilians including four children, amid clashes with federal troops. The accusations remain unverified.",
    "content": "Former South West State President Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed Laftagareen has accused Somalia's federal government and Türkiye of conducting airstrikes on Baidoa during intense clashes between his forces and troops loyal to the federal government. Laftagareen's office claimed that six missiles struck areas with civilians, resulting in casualties and property damage. The statement alleged that Turkish F-16 fighter jets participated in the bombardment. These allegations have not been independently verified, and no immediate response has been issued by the federal government or Turkish authorities.\n\nThe fighting in Baidoa marks a significant escalation in the longstanding political and military tensions between Laftagareen and the federal government. Laftagareen, who served as president of South West State until his term ended in 2023, has been in a standoff with Mogadishu over the extension of his mandate and the composition of regional forces. The federal government has accused him of destabilizing the region, while he has repeatedly claimed that federal troops are targeting his supporters.\n\nTürkiye has been a key ally of Somalia, providing military training and support to the Somali National Army. Turkish forces have been involved in training and equipping Somali troops, and Türkiye has also invested heavily in infrastructure and development projects in Mogadishu. The allegation that Turkish jets conducted airstrikes would, if confirmed, represent a major shift in Türkiye's role in Somalia's internal conflicts. However, Türkiye has not commented on the accusations.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the reported violence in Baidoa is deeply concerning. Baidoa is a major city in South West State and has been a focal point of political disputes and humanitarian crises. Many diaspora members have family ties to the region and have been actively involved in peacebuilding and development efforts. The accusations of foreign involvement in internal Somali conflicts could further polarize the diaspora, which already has diverse political allegiances. Additionally, any escalation in violence risks exacerbating the already dire humanitarian situation in the area, where many displaced persons reside.\n\nThe international community has yet to respond to these allegations. The African Union Transition Mission in Somalia (ATMIS) and other partners have consistently called for restraint and dialogue to resolve political disputes. The situation in Baidoa remains tense, with both sides reportedly mobilizing forces. Independent verification of the airstrikes is challenging due to restricted access to the area.\n\nAs the situation develops, it is crucial for all parties to prioritize the protection of civilians and engage in peaceful dialogue. The Somali diaspora can play a constructive role by advocating for calm and supporting humanitarian efforts. Ramadpay Daily News will continue to monitor this story and provide updates as more information becomes available.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-laftagareen-accuses-turkiye-six-airstrikes-baidoa/",
